1
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:04,760
Imagine having a DevOps teammate who never sleeps,

2
00:00:05,059 --> 00:00:07,340
someone who can debug a failing deployment,

3
00:00:07,539 --> 00:00:11,279
optimize Terraform modules, write Skaffold configuration,

4
00:00:11,579 --> 00:00:14,920
enter CI pipelines, and that too instantly.

5
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:18,340
That's what Gemini brings to DevOps on Google Cloud.

6
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:20,489
Let's just see how we can do all that.

7
00:00:20,690 --> 00:00:22,379
When you search for Gemini,

8
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:26,520
there are three Gemini tools that we can use as a DevOps.

9
00:00:26,659 --> 00:00:29,424
The first one is Gemini Code Assist.

10
00:00:29,665 --> 00:00:34,805
This is AI assisted coding inside VS Code and Cloud Workstation.

11
00:00:35,064 --> 00:00:37,444
The second one is a Gemini Cloud Assist.

12
00:00:37,705 --> 00:00:39,685
That is like an operational partner.

13
00:00:39,904 --> 00:00:42,415
And the third one is a Gemini CLI.

14
00:00:42,625 --> 00:00:44,525
Let's first start with the Code Assist.

15
00:00:44,705 --> 00:00:47,705
So come down here and search for Code Assist.

16
00:00:48,025 --> 00:00:51,895
Now inside the Gemini Code Assist, if you are doing for the first time,

17
00:00:52,064 --> 00:00:53,284
you need to enable.

18
00:00:54,049 --> 00:00:56,770
Now you have to select your billing account and let's

19
00:00:56,770 --> 00:00:59,439
say continue to Admin for Gemini page.

20
00:00:59,689 --> 00:01:03,060
Now select here the Standard, say Continue.

21
00:01:03,250 --> 00:01:07,900
In the number of license, I'm choosing just one license and saying Continue.

22
00:01:08,169 --> 00:01:09,720
Right now it is in trial mode,

23
00:01:10,010 --> 00:01:13,470
so that's why the cost is saying $0 for the first month.

24
00:01:13,610 --> 00:01:15,069
And let's say Complete purchase.

25
00:01:15,610 --> 00:01:18,089
Now this will enable a Gemini Code Assist.

26
00:01:18,410 --> 00:01:22,764
Now let's open up the VS Code and let's look for a plugin for Gemini.

27
00:01:23,044 --> 00:01:24,794
Here you see Gemini Code Assist.

28
00:01:25,014 --> 00:01:28,154
Let's install that app. Now this got installed successfully.

29
00:01:28,385 --> 00:01:32,654
Now on the right side bottom corner, you see an option for Gemini.

30
00:01:32,855 --> 00:01:33,584
Click on that.

31
00:01:33,815 --> 00:01:35,894
Here you see Gemini Code Assist.

32
00:01:36,135 --> 00:01:37,434
Let's say Enable.

33
00:01:37,775 --> 00:01:40,495
Now it's asking for sign in, so let's sign in.

34
00:01:40,894 --> 00:01:41,614
Say Open.

35
00:01:41,735 --> 00:01:42,684
It got logged in.

36
00:01:42,894 --> 00:01:46,254
Now it is asking me to select a cloud project, so I'm

37
00:01:46,254 --> 00:01:48,815
selecting that. Here I can see all of them.

38
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:54,580
I'm selecting my specific hpc‑1 project, and now you can see that we are

39
00:01:54,580 --> 00:01:57,959
successfully connected, and it can do a couple of things for me.

40
00:01:58,180 --> 00:02:00,050
I have to just type in here the prompt.

41
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:04,779
For example, I can say Find the issues in my file,

42
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:08,020
and then it can start suggesting to me that what could be the

43
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:11,050
potential error and what I can do best with that.

44
00:02:11,229 --> 00:02:15,740
Or maybe I can say here in my chat Generate a Cloud

45
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:20,710
Build pipeline that runs tests, builds a Docker image from a Dockerfile,

46
00:02:20,910 --> 00:02:25,210
scan it for vulnerabilities, and pushes it to the Artifact Registry.

47
00:02:25,389 --> 00:02:26,139
And let's go now.

48
00:02:26,389 --> 00:02:29,869
You can see that it has started creating the code for us, and it

49
00:02:29,869 --> 00:02:33,029
has written the cloudbuild.yaml completely.

50
00:02:33,270 --> 00:02:34,800
You have to just say Accept,

51
00:02:34,990 --> 00:02:38,789
and here you can see that code written for a Dockerfile as well and has

52
00:02:38,789 --> 00:02:41,610
given us all the commands that need to be get executed.

53
00:02:41,910 --> 00:02:44,960
So that's how Gemini Code Assist can help you to write the code,

54
00:02:44,990 --> 00:02:49,139
to find the issues, and to improve the performance of your specific code.

55
00:02:49,350 --> 00:02:54,429
Now on a similar line of the Code Assist, we have Gemini Cloud Assist as well.

56
00:02:54,630 --> 00:02:59,500
You can just search here Cloud Assist. And if it is not enabled,

57
00:02:59,710 --> 00:03:03,880
just click on Enable and enable the API for this Cloud Assist.

58
00:03:04,199 --> 00:03:07,229
Now let's say you go down to our Cloud Workstation.

59
00:03:07,699 --> 00:03:10,660
And on the top you can see Gemini Cloud Assist.

60
00:03:10,899 --> 00:03:14,860
Click on that icon. That will open up a window for Gemini Cloud Assist,

61
00:03:15,070 --> 00:03:17,589
and there you can start asking a question,

62
00:03:17,869 --> 00:03:18,369
saying,

63
00:03:18,550 --> 00:03:22,710
Explain my current cloud workstation. Even though I

64
00:03:22,710 --> 00:03:25,699
have misspelled the explain spelling,

65
00:03:25,830 --> 00:03:28,190
but still it is going to take it and give me the

66
00:03:28,190 --> 00:03:31,229
explanation about my current Cloud Workstations.

67
00:03:31,399 --> 00:03:34,000
It can help you to generate the Terraform scripts.

68
00:03:34,080 --> 00:03:37,839
It can help you to debug the issues, and many more.

69
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:41,660
Here you can see that it has created the entire summary for me,

70
00:03:41,919 --> 00:03:44,720
saying that the cluster name is this, location is this,

71
00:03:44,919 --> 00:03:48,220
and entire information that is there in my Cloud Workstation.

72
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:49,080
Similarly,

73
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:54,699
I can open up my logs and I can debug the logs using this Cloud Assist as well.

74
00:03:55,229 --> 00:03:59,449
Cloud Assist is available default in your entire cloud.

75
00:03:59,729 --> 00:04:02,880
Wherever you go on that screen, on the top,

76
00:04:03,009 --> 00:04:06,119
you can click on that icon of the Google Cloud Assist,

77
00:04:06,330 --> 00:04:07,770
and that window is available.

78
00:04:07,929 --> 00:04:10,820
You can start chatting and ask all your questions.

79
00:04:11,130 --> 00:04:12,710
Not only this, in fact,

80
00:04:13,169 --> 00:04:17,010
this Gemini Cloud Assist is also available in the CLI

81
00:04:17,290 --> 00:04:20,059
where we call it Gemini Cloud CLI.

82
00:04:20,298 --> 00:04:22,769
So let's open up our CLI, say Authorize.

83
00:04:23,019 --> 00:04:25,980
In the Cloud Shell, you have to just type in Gemini,

84
00:04:26,209 --> 00:04:30,619
and then the Gemini will appear where you can start typing your prompt

85
00:04:30,619 --> 00:04:33,700
and you can take all the help from the Google CLI.

86
00:04:33,980 --> 00:04:38,010
So that's how easy it is to use the Gemini in the CLI.

87
00:04:38,260 --> 00:04:42,700
So overall, we have a lot of AI assistance there for DevOps.

88
00:04:43,109 --> 00:04:44,140
As we have seen that,

89
00:04:44,339 --> 00:04:47,959
we have the AIs assistant in the form of Code Assist that

90
00:04:47,959 --> 00:04:49,799
you can integrate with the VS Code.

91
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:51,989
Cloud Assist automatically there,

92
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:58,260
which can help you for the operation purpose within your admin console itself.

93
00:04:58,399 --> 00:05:02,519
And the third one is you have the Gemini AI Assistant

94
00:05:02,519 --> 00:05:05,559
available within the Google Cloud CLI as well.
